---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/shopify-email';
const title = 'Shopify Email, now Messaging: evaluate native lifecycle work';
const desc = 'Evaluate Shopify Messaging email, Flow, recovery automations, channel eligibility and native sending costs without an assumed upgrade deadline.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Shopify operations · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Shopify Email, now Messaging: evaluate native lifecycle work</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Shopify Email is now Shopify Messaging. Native email is a practical option for catalog campaigns and lifecycle automation from Shopify admin, with additional messaging channels subject to setup and eligibility. Keep this route as the familiar entry point, but evaluate the current product rather than an older email-only feature list. The exercises below are proposed acceptance work, not performed tests or merchant results.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Start with the current app and eligible channels</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">Shopify’s current overview</a> describes email, SMS and WhatsApp campaigns and automations, identifies the former Shopify Email name and requires Shopify Network Intelligence. Install the Messaging app rather than assuming email exists without installation. Native access to store data reduces connector work; it does not remove audience, creative or operational decisions.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/requirements">General campaign requirements</a> include an active paid subscription, the Online Store channel and a payment provider. Trial and Pause and Build stores can prepare and test campaigns but cannot send them to subscribers. Check this before diagnosing a blocked launch as a broken template.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/sms/requirements">SMS eligibility and country rules</a> are separate from email. The current supported-country list includes Canada, the UK and the US excluding Puerto Rico; Austria and Spain are paused. Review the complete current list and content restrictions for the intended destinations. <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/sms/setup">SMS setup</a> requires toll-free registration for Canada and the US. An email-ready store is not automatically SMS-ready.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Native data plus Flow can support lifecycle work</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/requirements-considerations">Automation requirements</a> call for Basic or higher, Flow installed for full functionality and staff with Marketing and Flow App permissions. SMS and WhatsApp need their channels set up first. Multiple welcome automations can send duplicate messages; a disconnected channel returns its automations to draft. Audit app access and active automations before adding another workflow.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">The creation guide</a> includes browse, cart and checkout abandonment across email, SMS and WhatsApp, subject to channel availability. Welcome, post-purchase and customer appreciation templates are email-only and require desktop creation. The mobile app supports creating abandonment automations. This is materially more than manual sale broadcasts.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations">Shopify directs custom and third-party automation work to Flow</a>. Default marketing eligibility is channel subscription; abandoned-checkout email has a configurable all-customers exception. That setting is a product control, not evidence that any particular message is appropriate for every recipient. Inspect the selected audience and purpose before activation.</p>
      <p>Use native customer segments and catalog content to avoid redundant data copies where they meet the job. In a candle launch, preview a changed product title, sold-out variant and landing-page destination before scheduling. Decide whether the content should describe a collection or a specific SKU; do not assume that a previously drafted product block will remain correct through every catalog change.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Four proposed native data and recovery exercises</h2>
      <p>These cases assess configuration, not supplier performance. Save the expected entry, exclusion and message for each controlled profile. Keep sending disabled while inspecting the workflow, and use designated test recipients and orders for acceptance.</p>
      <p><strong>Checkout subscriber welcome:</strong> compare a subscriber who has already bought with a form subscriber who has not. Inspect the chosen welcome template’s actual entry event rather than assuming every subscription source triggers it. In Flow, check the available customer and order fields before designing a buyer branch. Repeat signup, correct the address and withdraw email permission during a wait. Accept only the intended welcome and offer eligibility, with no duplicate entry.</p>
      <p><strong>$94 abandoned checkout:</strong> distinguish a browse, cart and checkout event. Start with one native recovery template and inspect its audience setting. Complete the order before the delayed message, change the basket and revisit from another device. Check the recovered link and cancellation behavior. Inventory older checkout recovery and third-party queues so the same shopper does not receive several recovery offers for one checkout.</p>
      <p><strong>Digital gift card:</strong> use an order with different buyer and recipient identities. Inspect the actual fields exposed to the trigger and conditions before promising a gift exclusion. Essential delivery belongs to the correct recipient; marketing requires its own eligibility. Test mixed gift-card and physical-product baskets, refunds and late fulfillment. Record what the template handles directly and what requires a custom Flow condition.</p>
      <p><strong>100-day lapsed segment:</strong> treat 400 one-time buyers as a hypothetical candidate audience, not 400 eligible sends. Check fresh orders and current subscriptions before comparing full-price content with an optional incentive. If you schedule a segment campaign, inspect audience selection and send-time behavior rather than assuming it is a recurring event-triggered flow. For recurring winback, validate the required trigger, data and conditions in Flow.</p>
      <p>For an outcome experiment, randomize eligible customers to comparable variants and retain a holdout where feasible. Report net contribution after discounts, returns, message costs and labor over the same window. Opens and attributed purchases can help diagnose a journey; they do not prove incremental revenue or a reason to change providers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Count billable sends without inventing lost revenue</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/email/pricing/">Current email pricing</a> gives Basic, Grow, Advanced and Plus stores 10,000 free emails each calendar month. Beyond that, the first paid band is $1 USD per 1,000 through 300,000 sends, with lower rates at higher volumes. Ordinary email automations use the allowance; abandoned-checkout emails are free and excluded. Unused allowance does not roll over. SMS and WhatsApp costs are separate.</p>
      <p>For hypothetical billable email workloads, 3,000 monthly sends fit within the allowance. At 14,000, the 4,000 additional sends cost $4 USD; at 20,000, the 10,000 additional sends cost $10 USD in that first band. Count recipients, not campaigns: four campaigns to 3,500 addresses total 14,000 sends before other billable automation activity. These figures exclude the Shopify plan, labor and other channels.</p>
      <p>A $94 basket is not $94 of contribution. An illustrative 15% discount is $14.10, leaving $79.90 before tax, product cost, fulfillment, returns and messaging. Model those costs before deciding whether a recovery offer is worthwhile. An assumed recovery percentage multiplied by abandoned revenue is not measured lost profit.</p>
      <p>Budget the work of creating and maintaining Flow conditions, segments, templates, channel registrations and reporting. Compare that operating cost with a dated alternative proposal at the same workload. There is no automatic graduation at 300 or 400 orders, six months of trading or a particular subscriber count.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Keep native operation when it passes the actual job</h2>
      <p>Native operation suits a team that values working in admin and can maintain the required data conditions and approvals. Investigate a different platform when a specific required trigger, channel destination, reporting export, collaboration control or service deliverable cannot be implemented acceptably. Write that requirement down before comparing product lists.</p>
      <p>The actual <a class="link" href="/apps/sendlane">Sendlane review</a> centers on supported account continuity, named service ownership and migration acceptance after acquisition. This page centers on native configuration and Flow evidence. The <a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy review</a> follows onsite capture and offers into eligible messaging. These are distinct operating jobs, not an ordered ladder from starter to premium.</p>
      <p>Existing <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-shopify-email">Omnisend</a> and <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-shopify-email">Klaviyo</a> comparisons can help frame questions, but their supplier claims have not been revalidated by this review. No extra sender is required merely because it appears in this directory.</p>
      <p>If switching, inventory native campaigns, draft and active automations, Flow workflows and scheduled messages before enabling the replacement. Preserve suppression and permission evidence, authenticate the sender and assign one owner per trigger. Test a late purchase and withdrawal across the handoff; do not leave both systems sending the same recovery sequence.</p>
      <p>Review draft automations after channel changes, failed runs, stale product links, duplicate welcomes and unexplained audience changes. Record who can pause a campaign and who fixes Flow conditions. Revisit the tool decision when these operating requirements change, rather than imposing an arbitrary removal date on a working native program.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
